WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.422
A busy server holds thousands of open connections, and most of them are quiet at any moment.

2
00:00:08.422 --> 00:00:14.020
A thread for each one would park thousands of threads, at about a megabyte of memory each.

3
00:00:14.233 --> 00:00:23.392
Jetty splits the work into three jobs, and every thread comes from one pool: a QueuedThreadPool, with at least 8 threads and at most 200.

4
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:26.391
The connector leases a few of them for good.

5
00:00:26.391 --> 00:00:27.977
One becomes the acceptor.

6
00:00:27.977 --> 00:00:30.895
It calls accept, and hands each new socket on.

7
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.748
On 4 CPUs, two more become selectors.

8
00:00:33.748 --> 00:00:41.908
Each one runs a select loop, epoll on Linux, and watches many sockets at once for readiness: up to thousands each.

9
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:46.333
The pool also keeps a few idle threads ready, called reserved threads.

10
00:00:46.333 --> 00:00:48.449
And on the right sits your handler.

11
00:00:48.633 --> 00:00:49.949
Follow one request.

12
00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:51.196
A client connects.

13
00:00:51.196 --> 00:00:55.352
The acceptor accepts the socket, and hands it to a selector.

14
00:00:55.533 --> 00:00:57.336
The client sends its request.

15
00:00:57.336 --> 00:01:03.675
The selector sees that socket is readable, and makes a task: parse the request, and call your handler.

16
00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:06.177
Your handler is declared blocking.

17
00:01:06.177 --> 00:01:08.052
Which thread runs that task?

18
00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:12.341
Usually, the selector thread itself.

19
00:01:12.341 --> 00:01:15.419
First a reserved thread takes over the watching.

20
00:01:15.419 --> 00:01:21.381
Then the selector thread runs your handler, while the request is still warm in its CPU cache.

21
00:01:21.567 --> 00:01:24.560
Jetty calls this Execute-Produce-Consume.

22
00:01:25.567 --> 00:01:31.782
With no reserved thread free, the task goes into the pool's queue, and a pool thread picks it up.

23
00:01:31.782 --> 00:01:36.524
That is Produce-Execute-Consume: a hand-off and cold caches, but no stall.

24
00:01:37.433 --> 00:01:41.413
A handler that declares itself non-blocking skips the hand-off.

25
00:01:41.413 --> 00:01:45.836
The selector thread runs it on the spot, just like a Netty event loop.

26
00:01:45.836 --> 00:01:47.352
That is Produce-Consume.

27
00:01:47.533 --> 00:01:49.023
But that is a promise.

28
00:01:49.023 --> 00:01:55.184
Break it with a JDBC call, and every other socket on that selector waits behind your query.

29
00:01:56.567 --> 00:01:59.332
The promise is your handler's superclass.

30
00:01:59.332 --> 00:02:02.637
Handler.Abstract.NonBlocking says: I never block.

31
00:02:02.637 --> 00:02:04.998
Handler.Abstract says: I may block.

32
00:02:04.998 --> 00:02:07.089
Use it for anything that waits.

33
00:02:07.867 --> 00:02:16.289
The rule: acceptors take connections, selectors notice ready sockets, and Jetty picks who runs your handler from what it declares.

34
00:02:16.289 --> 00:02:17.779
So declare it honestly.

35
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:18.920
A quick check.

36
00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:22.997
Set the reserved threads to zero, and call a blocking handler.

37
00:02:22.997 --> 00:02:24.377
Which thread runs it?
